# Design Document

## Overview

This design introduces a second path root — the **workspace** (the worktree an agent is actually in) — alongside the existing **workflow root** (where the shared `.spec-workflow` lives), and threads it to every operation that touches code while leaving `.spec-workflow` operations on the shared root.

The change has four layers:

1. **Resolution** — one function in `git-utils.ts` decides both roots from env, flag, cwd inference, or the path argument.
2. **Propagation** — `ToolContext` and both runner option types carry two named roots, and the construction sites are *type-checked* so the compiler actually enforces it.
3. **Consumption** — `review-task`, `task-diff`, and `typecheck` pick the correct root per operation, partition file lists by which root resolved them, and carry an execution-context object into the review prompt.
4. **Cost containment** — `ProjectManager` shares three of its four per-workflow-root components so N worktrees do not multiply the dashboard's work, and registry mutation moves behind an exclusive lock.

Three new modules are added (`TaskStateStore`, `execution-context`, `registry-lock`); everything else extends existing files.

## Steering Document Alignment

### Technical Standards (tech.md)
N/A — this project has no `.spec-workflow/steering/` documents. The design follows conventions observed in the codebase: pure functions in `src/core/` with side effects confined to explicit read/write helpers, discriminated-union result types over thrown errors for expected failure states (`TypecheckResult`), `warnOnce` for non-fatal diagnostics, and temp-file-plus-rename for JSON state writes.

### Project Structure (structure.md)
N/A — no `structure.md`. The design follows the existing layout: shared logic in `src/core/`, MCP tool handlers in `src/tools/`, dashboard server and runners in `src/dashboard/`, tests colocated in `__tests__/`.

### Design System (design-system.md)
N/A — this spec has no visual surface. Requirement 10 changes how dashboard events fan out internally, but the WebSocket message shape is deliberately unchanged, so **no frontend code changes** and the committed `dist` bundle does not need rebuilding.

## Code Reuse Analysis

### Existing Components to Leverage

- **`resolveGitRoot` / `resolveGitWorkspaceRoot`** (`src/core/git-utils.ts:18-74`): the relative-path normalization at `:60-66` is reused by both the new raw common-dir helper and by `ensureGitExcludeEntry`. `resolveGitRoot` keeps its current contract and its `SPEC_WORKFLOW_SHARED_ROOT` override.
- **`PathUtils`** (`src/core/path-utils.ts`): all `.spec-workflow` path construction continues through `getSpecPath`, `getWorkflowRoot`, `getSteeringPath`. Docker translation via `PathUtils.translatePath` stays the single entry point.
- **`TaskReviewManager`** (`src/core/task-review-manager.ts:33-97`): already owns per-task sidecar files and sanitizes task IDs (`:72-73`). `TaskStateStore` follows the same file-per-task convention.
- **`warnOnce`**: reused for the override warning (R3 AC 5) and the warned file drops (R4 AC 4).
- **`TypecheckResult`** (`src/core/typecheck.ts:15-45`): a discriminated union with a closed `reason` union. New reasons extend the existing `unavailable` arm.
- **`parseTscOutput`'s `listFiles`** (`src/core/typecheck.ts:316-321`): already returned and currently used only for coverage. Reused as the dependency-resolution probe, so R7 AC 3 costs no extra work.
- **`readRegistry`'s corruption recovery** (`src/core/project-registry.ts:125-139`): retained; the lock prevents corruption, this remains the backstop.

### Integration Points

- **`src/index.ts:115`, `:166-175`, `:180-181`**: flag registration, argument filter, and the sole CLI resolution site.
- **`src/server.ts:93-97`, `:135`**: typed `ToolContext` construction and `setupHandlers` signature.
- **`src/dashboard/project-manager.ts:140-201`**: shared-resource lookup with serialization.
- **`src/dashboard/multi-server.ts:346`, `:401`, `:438`, `:828`, `:852`, `:1404`, `:1791`, `:1853`**: event handlers, route root selection, and diff-base recording.
- **`src/dashboard/job-scheduler.ts:161-190`**: iteration deduped by workflow root.
- **`src/tools/log-implementation.ts:307`**: the attribution writer.

## Architecture

Resolution happens once, at process start, and flows outward. No module below `index.ts` re-derives roots from git except the dashboard's manual-add path, which is deliberately independent (R1 AC 9, R2 AC 7) and shares only the identity normalization.

### Modular Design Principles

- **Single File Responsibility**: root *resolution* lives only in `git-utils.ts`; root *selection* lives at each call site and is visible there; per-task state lives only in `TaskStateStore`; registry mutual exclusion lives only in `registry-lock.ts`.
- **Component Isolation**: the execution-context builder is a pure function over already-computed results — no I/O, testable without a repository.
- **Service Layer Separation**: `TaskStateStore` is storage only; policy lives in `review-task`.
- **Utility Modularity**: the dependency probe is a pure predicate over `listFiles`, separable from the `tsc` invocation.

```mermaid
graph TD
    CLI[index.ts] -->|resolveWorkspaceRoots| GU[git-utils]
    GU -->|two roots| SRV[server.ts]
    SRV -->|ToolContext typed| RT[review-task]

    DASH[multi-server routes] -->|workflowRoot| AH[adversarialReviewHandler]
    AH -->|writes disclosure| SCAF[scaffold prompt file]
    DASH -->|both roots| TRR[TaskReviewRunner]
    TRR -->|cwd = workspace| AGENT[spawned agent]

    RT -->|workspace-resolved only| TD[task-diff]
    RT -->|workspace| TC[typecheck]
    RT -->|workflowRoot| SPEC[.spec-workflow reads]
    RT -->|read base + attribution| TSS[TaskStateStore]
    LOG[log-implementation] -->|writes attribution| TSS

    TD --> EC[buildExecutionContext]
    TC --> EC
    TSS --> EC
    EC -->|data.executionContext| TRR
    TRR -->|buildPrompt| AGENT

    PM[ProjectManager] -->|mutex| SR[SharedWorkflowResources]
    SR --> PARSER[SpecParser]
    SR --> WATCH[SpecWatcher]
    SR --> ARCH[ArchiveService]
    PM -->|per entry, NOT shared| APPR[ApprovalStorage]

    REG[project-registry] --> LOCK[registry-lock]
```

## Components and Interfaces

### 1. `resolveWorkspaceRoots` — `src/core/git-utils.ts`

- **Purpose:** Single decision point for both roots (R1, R2).
- **Interfaces:**
  ```ts
  export type WorkspaceSource = 'env' | 'flag' | 'inference' | 'argument';

  export interface ResolvedRoots {
    workspacePath: string;       // realpath-normalized
    workflowRootPath: string;
    source: WorkspaceSource;
  }

  export function resolveWorkspaceRoots(opts: {
    configuredPath: string;
    cwd: string;
    dashboardMode: boolean;      // R2 AC 6 — distinct from noInference
    noInference: boolean;
    noSharedWorktreeSpecs: boolean;
  }): ResolvedRoots;

  export function normalizeIdentityPath(p: string): string;
  export function resolveGitCommonDirAbsolute(cwd: string): string | null;
  ```
- **Workspace precedence:** `SPEC_WORKFLOW_WORKSPACE` (skipped when `dashboardMode`) → `noInference` → inference → `configuredPath`. `dashboardMode` is separate from `noInference` because it must suppress the *env override* as well, and the env var outranks the flag (R2 AC 3/6/7).
- **Workflow root:** when `noSharedWorktreeSpecs` is set, `workflowRootPath = workspacePath` — including when the workspace came from inference or the env override (R2 AC 5). Otherwise `resolveGitRoot(configuredPath)` (R2 AC 4). This ordering is what keeps the flag's meaning intact; deriving from `configuredPath` under the flag would place `.spec-workflow` in the main repo and break the existing e2e suite.
- **`resolveGitCommonDirAbsolute`** wraps `git rev-parse --git-common-dir` and applies the normalization at `git-utils.ts:60-66`. The raw command returns the bare string `.git` from a repository root, so every consumer must go through this (R1 AC 6). It deliberately does **not** consult `SPEC_WORKFLOW_SHARED_ROOT` (R1 AC 5). Component 8 uses it too.
- **Failure:** every git call is wrapped; any throw falls back to `configuredPath`.

### 2. Typed two-root tool context — `src/types.ts`, `src/server.ts`, `src/tools/root-selection.ts` (new)

- **Purpose:** Carry both roots and make the requirement *enforced* rather than declared (R3).
- **Interfaces:**
  ```ts
  export interface ToolContext {
    projectPath: string;      // workflow root
    workspacePath: string;    // REQUIRED
    dashboardUrl?: string;
    lang?: string;
  }

  export function selectRoots(
    args: { projectPath?: string },
    context: ToolContext
  ): { projectPath: string; workspacePath: string };
  ```
- **The enforcement is the typing, not the required field.** `src/server.ts:93-97` currently builds an unannotated object literal and passes it to `setupHandlers(context: any)` (`:135`). Adding a required field to `ToolContext` produces **zero** compiler errors there — at the one construction site whose omission reproduces the defect this spec exists to fix. Two changes make the requirement real:
  - annotate the literal `const context: ToolContext = {…}`
  - change `setupHandlers(context: any)` → `setupHandlers(context: ToolContext)`
  Without both, R3 AC 1 is a convention that the primary path silently ignores.
- **Test fixtures** (R3 AC 4): `src/tools/__tests__/spec-index.test.ts:79` passes `{} as ToolContext`; the assertion must be replaced with a real value or the check is defeated there too. Four annotated fixtures (`prompts/__tests__/create-steering-doc.test.ts:5`, `tools/__tests__/decomposition-guide.test.ts:6`, `tools/__tests__/projectPath.test.ts:10,41,74,134`) will fail compilation until updated — which is the mechanism working.
- **`selectRoots`** centralises R3 AC 5/6: with an override, both roots derive from it *and* `warnOnce` fires naming the override and the discarded `context.workspacePath`.

### 3. `TaskStateStore` — `src/core/task-state-store.ts` (new)

- **Purpose:** Per-task, **per-workspace** record holding the diff base and workspace attribution (R5 AC 1/9, R8).
- **Interfaces:**
  ```ts
  export interface TaskState {
    taskId: string;
    workspaceKey: string;              // sha1 of realpath(workspacePath)
    workspacePath?: string;
    workspaceSource?: 'context' | 'override';
    baseCommit?: string;
    baseRecordedAt?: string;
    headCommit?: string;               // R8 AC 1 — HEAD at logging time
    lastLoggedAt?: string;
  }

  export class TaskStateStore {
    constructor(specPath: string);
    get(taskId: string, workspacePath: string): Promise<TaskState | null>;
    listForTask(taskId: string): Promise<TaskState[]>;
    recordBase(taskId: string, workspacePath: string, commit: string): Promise<void>;
    recordAttribution(taskId: string, workspacePath: string,
                      source: 'context' | 'override', headCommit: string): Promise<void>;
  }
  ```
- **Storage:** `<specPath>/.task-state/<sanitized-taskId>.<workspaceKey>.json`. **Keying by workspace is load-bearing.** A single file per task on the shared workflow root means worktree B reviewing a task started in worktree A reads A's base commit — and because linked worktrees share one object database, every resolvability check on that foreign SHA succeeds. That reproduces the exact wrong-checkout defect this spec exists to eliminate. `listForTask` supports the mismatch disclosure by finding records from other workspaces.
- **Concurrency (R11 AC 8):** per-file writes use the same lock helper as Component 7, since two worktrees can write the same task's records concurrently through the shared root.
- **Writers:** `recordBase` ← the dashboard task-status endpoint (Component 9). `recordAttribution` ← `log-implementation` (Component 11). R8 AC 2 requires exactly one attribution writer.

### 4. Execution context — `src/core/execution-context.ts` (new)

- **Purpose:** One object naming the run's condition, carried into the prompt (R9).
- **Interfaces:**
  ```ts
  export type DiffBaseState =
    | { kind: 'recorded'; commit: string }
    | { kind: 'baseline-no-base' }                    // expected; NOT degraded
    | { kind: 'degraded-base-rejected'; commit: string }
    | { kind: 'degraded-diff-rejected'; detail: string };

  export interface ExecutionContext {
    workspacePath: string;
    workflowRoot: string;
    diffBase: DiffBaseState;
    typecheck: { status: TypecheckResult['status']; reason?: string };
    attribution:
      | { state: 'match' }
      | { state: 'mismatch'; recorded: string; reviewing: string }
      | { state: 'unknown' };
    degraded: boolean;
  }
  ```
- **Baseline versus degraded (R9 AC 4/5).** `degraded` excludes `baseline-no-base`. R5 AC 10 establishes that no base is recorded whenever an agent edits `tasks.md` directly — the dominant path — so folding that into `degraded` would set the flag on essentially every review forever, and a directive that always fires trains agents to skip the block. Baseline is stated factually; only a rejected base, an unavailable typecheck, a rejected diff, or an attribution mismatch triggers the qualify-your-verdict directive.
- **Channel ownership (R9 AC 6).** `buildReviewMethodology` already emits directives for empty diffs, rejected diffs, partial coverage, and unavailable typechecks. To avoid two channels contradicting each other: the **methodology** keeps ownership of what the reviewer should *do*; the **execution context** owns what the run's *conditions were* (which roots, which base, which provenance) and adds no duplicate directive where a methodology constant already covers the case.
- **Field name (R9 AC 1):** returned as `data.executionContext`, which `TaskReviewRunner` destructures alongside its existing five fields. Not `projectContext` — that object is discarded at `task-review-runner.ts:110`.
- **Adversarial shape (R9 AC 7):** an adversarial run has no task, base, or attribution. `renderAdversarialContext(workspacePath, workflowRoot)` emits the two-field form, and it is written into the scaffold by `adversarialReviewHandler` — not by `AdversarialRunner`, whose entire prompt is a one-line pointer to that scaffold (`adversarial-runner.ts:110`).

### 5. Runner option contracts — `task-review-runner.ts`, `adversarial-runner.ts`

- **Purpose:** Replace the single overloaded `projectPath` (R6).
  ```ts
  interface RunOptions {
    projectId: string; specName: string; taskId: string;
    workflowRoot: string;    // getSpecPath → TaskReviewManager, ToolContext.projectPath
    workspacePath: string;   // spawn cwd, ToolContext.workspacePath
    model?: string; cli?: string; cliArgs?: string[];
  }
  ```
- **Call-site mapping** — three direct uses of `opts.projectPath`: `:95` `getSpecPath` → workflowRoot (the `TaskReviewManager` at `:96` consumes the resulting `specPath`, not the option); `:100` `ToolContext` → both; `:144` → `:365` spawn `cwd` → workspacePath.
- **Env scrubbing** at `task-review-runner.ts:368` and `adversarial-runner.ts:146`: `SPEC_WORKFLOW_WORKSPACE` and `SPEC_WORKFLOW_SHARED_ROOT` are deleted from the inherited env and set explicitly to the job's roots (R2 AC 8). `SPEC_WORKFLOW_HOST_PATH_PREFIX` / `_CONTAINER_PATH_PREFIX` are preserved unchanged (R2 AC 9).

### 6. `SharedWorkflowResources` — `src/dashboard/project-manager.ts`

- **Purpose:** Keep dashboard cost constant in the number of worktrees (R10).
  ```ts
  interface SharedWorkflowResources {
    parser: SpecParser;
    watcher: SpecWatcher;
    archiveService: SpecArchiveService;
    projectIds: Set<string>;
  }
  private sharedByRoot = new Map<string, SharedWorkflowResources>();  // keyed on UNTRANSLATED root
  private sharedMutex = new Mutex();
  ```
- **`ApprovalStorage` is excluded (R10 AC 2).** Its `fileResolutionPath` is per-workspace instance state consumed by a private resolver reached from five public methods (`updateApproval`, `captureSnapshot`, `getCurrentFileContent`, `compareSnapshots`, `createRevision`). Sharing one instance forces the constructing project's workspace onto every entry on that root — and because the workflow root is candidate #2, resolution usually still finds *a* file: the wrong one, from a sibling worktree, silently. That is the defect this spec exists to fix, reintroduced by the component meant to pay for it. It also has no answer at the `approval-change` broadcast handler (`multi-server.ts:401`), which has no single requesting project by construction. One instance per project entry preserves R11 AC 10; its cost is a debounced watcher over one directory, which the cost argument never depended on.
- **Serialization (R10 AC 6).** `syncWithRegistry` is invoked from two unguarded chokidar handlers (`:80-86`) with no re-entrancy guard, and `addProject` awaits `watcher.start()` before publishing state at `:193`. Without a mutex, two registrations on one root each observe an empty map and each construct a full component set — orphaning one with live watchers and no handle to stop them. Acquire `sharedMutex` around lookup-or-create and around teardown.
- **Construction failure (R10 AC 7):** insert into `sharedByRoot` only after both `start()` calls resolve; on throw, delete the partial entry so the next project retries rather than inheriting a dead watcher.
- **Key on the untranslated root (R10 AC 1):** `sharedByRoot` is keyed on `entry.workflowRootPath`, translating only for construction. Keying on the translated path risks two distinct host roots collapsing onto one container path and silently sharing each other's specs.
- **Event fan-out (R10 AC 3/4):** the *internal* `ProjectManager` event carries `projectIds: string[]`; the **WebSocket message shape is unchanged**. Each `multi-server` handler (`:346`, `:401`, `:438`, and the remaining forwarded-event handlers) loops the ids and calls `broadcastToProject(id, { …, projectId: id })`. This is required: `broadcastToProject` filters by `connection.projectId` and the client re-checks the field, so a single message carrying an array would match no subscriber. R10 AC 3's cost goal is met by reading the tree **once** and fanning out, not by sending once. Per-project debounce maps keyed by `projectId` are rekeyed accordingly. **Net effect: no frontend change and no `dist` rebuild.**
- **Job scheduler (R10 AC 8):** `executeJob` dedupes by workflow root before running the three cleanup passes.

### 7. Registry mutual exclusion — `src/core/registry-lock.ts` (new), `src/core/project-registry.ts`

- **Purpose:** Make R11 AC 2 true rather than probable.
- **Why not optimistic retry.** Re-reading and comparing before rename narrows a TOCTOU window it cannot close — there is no compare-and-swap in `rename`. The decisive case needs no interleaving luck at all: with no `activeProjects.json` present, every starting process reads `ENOENT`, observes "absent" at every check, and renames its own complete file. The last rename wins and the other registrations vanish with no retry and no error — R11 AC 2 failing on first registration.
- **Design:** `withRegistryLock(fn)` acquires via `fs.open(lockPath, 'wx')`, writes `{pid, hostname, acquiredAt}`, and releases in `finally`. A lock whose `acquiredAt` exceeds a staleness window (and whose pid is not alive locally) is broken and reacquired. Acquisition retries within a **time budget** rather than a fixed attempt count (R11 AC 4), sized to cover a realistic simultaneous start of a dozen worktrees on a slow `$HOME`.
- **All four writers wrapped (R11 AC 5):** `registerProject`, `cleanupStaleProjects` (`:297-320`), `unregisterProject` (`:233-255`), `unregisterProjectById` (`:260-264`). The last two delete and are reachable from the dashboard's removal route via `removeProjectById`.
- **Unique temp name (R11 AC 1):** `${registryPath}.${pid}.${counter}.tmp`. This — not rename atomicity — is what stops two writers interleaving bytes into one shared temp file and installing a syntactically invalid registry.
- **Failure policy (R11 AC 6):** on budget exhaustion, log prominently and **continue unregistered**. `registerProject` is awaited in `initialize` (`server.ts:76`) before the transport connects (`:105`), so throwing kills the MCP handshake and the user sees "server failed to start" with the real cause buried in stderr — worse than a working but dashboard-invisible server.

### 8. Typecheck honesty — `src/core/typecheck.ts`

- **Purpose:** Never return a verdict the run cannot support (R7).
- **Direct dependency probe (R7 AC 3/4), not a diagnostic-shape heuristic:**
  ```ts
  function workspaceDepsResolvable(workspace: string, listFiles: string[]): boolean
  ```
  True when `listFiles` contains any path under the workspace's `node_modules`, or `<workspace>/node_modules/typescript/lib` exists. `listFiles` is already parsed and returned (`:316-321`), so the signal costs nothing.
  A ratio test over `TS2307` was considered and rejected: it cannot distinguish a broken `node_modules` from a large legitimate file move (discarding real type errors in the latter), it misses small worktrees under any count floor, and `TS2688` is unreachable anyway because it is emitted without a file position while `DIAGNOSTIC_HEADER_RE` (`:272`) requires one.
- **Placement:** the probe runs on the raw parse result immediately after `parseTscOutput` (`:168`) and short-circuits to `unavailable`, **before** `await postProcess(…)` at `:178`. `postProcess` realpaths every diagnostic file and every `listFiles` entry; running it and then discarding the result wastes thousands of `fs` calls.
- **New `unavailable` reasons:** `'workspace-deps-unresolvable'`, `'no-tsconfig-in-workspace'`.
- **Methodology text (R7 AC 6):** `R4_6B_TYPECHECK_UNAVAILABLE` (`review-task.ts:724-725`) enumerates reason strings verbatim and is byte-pinned by a two-way drift test (`review-task.test.ts:1293`) that compares against **another spec's** gitignored `requirements.md` — so the test is skipped in a fresh clone and live on the maintainer's machine. Decision: extend the constant with the new reasons and **relax the drift test** to compare only the constants this spec does not touch, removing the cross-spec dependency rather than editing an unrelated spec's approved document.
- **Cache & exclude:** cache moves to `<workspacePath>/.spec-workflow/.cache`. `ensureGitignoreEntry` is replaced by `ensureGitExcludeEntry`, which writes to `join(resolveGitCommonDirAbsolute(workspace), 'info', 'exclude')` — untracked, shared across worktrees, effective without a commit (R7 AC 10). It **must** go through Component 1's normalizer: the raw command returns `.git` from a repository root, so joining unnormalized writes under `process.cwd()`, the client's launch directory (R7 AC 11). When the workspace is not a git repository, `info/exclude` is absent, or the repository is bare, the exclusion is skipped without failing the typecheck (R7 AC 12). This adds the first `git` invocation to `typecheck.ts`, once per run.

### 9. Diff base — `src/core/task-diff.ts`, `src/dashboard/multi-server.ts:1404`

- **Purpose:** Per-task diff semantics (R5).
- **Recording:** the task-status endpoint records the base on the transition to `in-progress`. The producer is an explicit `git rev-parse HEAD` run with `cwd` set to the project's **workspace** path; if that read fails, nothing is recorded and the fallback applies (R5 AC 2).
- **Hit rate, stated honestly (R5 AC 10):** this endpoint is the only server-mediated task-start hook that exists. Agents that edit `tasks.md` directly — the dominant path — never reach it, so a base is recorded only for dashboard-driven status changes. The component is retained because it is correct when it fires and because the deferred task-status tool (`d-75761c78`) is what would make it fire generally; the baseline arm of Component 4 exists precisely so the common no-base case reads as normal rather than degraded.
- **Validation (R5 AC 4):** `git merge-base --is-ancestor <base> HEAD`, not `git rev-parse --verify`. Linked worktrees share one object database, so `--verify` succeeds on any commit from any sibling branch and cannot detect a foreign or rewritten base.
- **Diffing:** recorded and ancestral → `git diff <base> -- <files>`. No record for this workspace → `HEAD`, `baseline-no-base`. Record present but not an ancestor → `HEAD`, `degraded-base-rejected`.
- **Rejection field (R4 AC 8):** `computeTaskDiff`'s `!ok` branch (`task-diff.ts:53-55`) currently returns an empty diff indistinguishable from a genuinely clean tree, which downstream classifies as the benign "already committed" case. It gains a `rejected: { reason }` field that feeds `degraded-diff-rejected`.

### 10. `review-task` root selection — `src/tools/review-task.ts`

- **Purpose:** Apply the split and partition the results (R4).
- **Two-root resolution lives in `validateAllFiles`, not at `:361`.** Line 361 is a pure `path.resolve`; the filesystem probe the rule depends on is the `safeRealpath` at `:64`. `validateAllFiles` takes both roots and returns a partition:
  ```ts
  export function validateAllFiles(input: unknown, roots: { workspacePath: string; workflowRoot: string })
    : { workspaceFiles: string[]; workflowFiles: string[] };
  ```
  It is exported and directly unit-tested, so this is a test-surface change.
- **Resolution order:** workspace first, workflow root second (R4 AC 3). A path resolving against neither is dropped **with `warnOnce`**, closing the silent `continue` at `:65-67` (R4 AC 4).
- **Containment (R4 AC 6):** accept under `workspacePath`, **or** under `join(workflowRoot, '.spec-workflow')` — not under `workflowRoot` itself. In a nested layout (`~/repo/worktrees/a`) the workflow root is an ancestor of every sibling worktree, so accepting it would admit another agent's checkout during this review.
- **Partition consumers:** only `workspaceFiles` reach `computeTaskDiff` (R4 AC 7) — passing a workflow-root path as pathspec with `cwd` set to the workspace makes git reject the whole invocation, discarding the entire diff. Only `workspaceFiles` count toward typecheck coverage (R4 AC 9); workflow-root paths cannot appear in `--listFiles` and would otherwise flip every documentation review to partial coverage. Both lists still populate `filesToReview`.
- **Unchanged on the workflow root:** `:285`, `:345`, `:381`, `:432`, `:556`.

### 11. Attribution writer — `src/tools/log-implementation.ts`

- **Purpose:** The single writer R8 AC 2 requires.
- **Behaviour:** after a successful log write, call `TaskStateStore.recordAttribution` with the resolved workspace (via `selectRoots`), the `'context' | 'override'` provenance from R3 AC 5, and `git rev-parse HEAD` run in the workspace as `headCommit` (R8 AC 1). A failed HEAD read records the workspace without the commit rather than failing the log.

### 12. CLI flag registration — `src/index.ts`

- **Purpose:** R1 AC 11, which had no owner in the previous draft.
- **Behaviour:** add `--no-workspace-inference` to `validFlags` (`:115`) and to the argument filter (`:166-175`). Omitting the `validFlags` entry does not merely misparse the path — the validation loop at `:116-128` **throws** `Unknown option`, so the server refuses to start.

## Data Models

### TaskState — `<specPath>/.task-state/<sanitized-taskId>.<workspaceKey>.json`
```
- taskId: string
- workspaceKey: string                   (sha1 of realpath(workspacePath), 16 chars)
- workspacePath: absolute path | undefined
- workspaceSource: 'context' | 'override' | undefined
- baseCommit: string | undefined         (40-char SHA)
- baseRecordedAt: ISO-8601 | undefined
- headCommit: string | undefined         (R8 AC 1)
- lastLoggedAt: ISO-8601 | undefined
```
No file for the reviewing workspace means **unknown** attribution and a baseline diff base. Every task logged before this release is in that state.

### RegistryLock — `<globalDir>/activeProjects.lock`
```
- pid: number
- hostname: string
- acquiredAt: ISO-8601
```

### ExecutionContext / ResolvedRoots
In-memory only; shapes defined in Components 1 and 4.

## Error Handling

1. **Git unavailable during resolution** — each call wrapped; falls back to the configured path; never throws out of `parseArguments`.
2. **`realpath` fails on the workspace** — use the un-normalized absolute path, log once (R1 AC 8).
3. **Registry lock budget exhausted** — log prominently, continue unregistered, MCP handshake unaffected (R11 AC 6).
4. **Recorded base is not an ancestor of HEAD** — fall back to `HEAD`, `degraded-base-rejected`, directive fires.
5. **Workspace dependencies unresolvable** — `unavailable: 'workspace-deps-unresolvable'` before `postProcess`; never `success` with fabricated `TS2307`s.
6. **Logged path resolves against neither root** — dropped with `warnOnce`; if every path drops, `filesToReview` is empty and the context says so.
7. **`git diff` rejects a pathspec** — `rejected` field set, surfaced as `degraded-diff-rejected` rather than misread as an empty diff.
8. **Shared component construction fails** — partial entry deleted under the mutex; the next project on that root retries (R10 AC 7).
9. **Worktree directory removed while registered** — note that `addProject` reads only the workflow root, so registration itself does **not** fail. The failure surfaces downstream: the runner's `spawn({ cwd: workspacePath })` throws `ENOENT`, and `runGit` returns `ok: false` (now visible via the `rejected` field rather than silently empty). Tests target those sites, not registration.

## Testing Strategy

### Unit Testing
- `resolveWorkspaceRoots`: full precedence matrix including `dashboardMode` suppressing the env var, and `--no-shared-worktree-specs` keeping the workflow root on the workspace. Exercised against real temp repositories, since relative-vs-absolute common-dir behaviour is the thing most likely to regress.
- `resolveGitCommonDirAbsolute`: from repo root (bare `.git`), from a subdirectory (`../../.git`), from a linked worktree, and in a non-git directory.
- `selectRoots`: override present/absent; warning fires once per distinct override.
- `validateAllFiles`: partition correctness, warned drops, containment accepting `.spec-workflow` above the workspace while rejecting a sibling worktree in a nested layout.
- `workspaceDepsResolvable`: resolvable, missing `node_modules`, and a large legitimate rename that must stay `success`.
- `buildExecutionContext`: `baseline-no-base` must **not** set `degraded`; each degraded trigger must.
- `TaskStateStore`: per-workspace keying — two workspaces, same task, independent records; concurrent writes.
- `registry-lock`: mutual exclusion, stale-lock breaking, budget exhaustion.

### Integration Testing
- `handlePrepare` with workspace ≠ workflow root: diff receives only workspace-resolved paths; typecheck coverage excludes workflow-root paths; spec/steering/settings on the workflow root; `.spec-workflow` logged paths still appear in `filesToReview`.
- Registry: N concurrent registrations against a **nonexistent** registry file yield N entries — the case optimistic retry cannot satisfy. All four writers under contention.
- `TaskReviewRunner`: two-field contract; `getSpecPath` on the workflow root; spawn `cwd` on the workspace; env scrubbed; `data.executionContext` present in the built prompt (the `:110` destructure regression guard).
- Adversarial route: handler receives the workflow root (`:828`), runner receives the workspace (`:852`), disclosure present in the scaffold file.
- `ProjectManager`: concurrent `addProject` on one root yields exactly one component set; `ApprovalStorage` remains one per entry; teardown only when the last entry leaves; one tree read fanned out per subscriber with the WebSocket shape unchanged.
- Job scheduler: two entries on one root produce one cleanup pass and an uninflated `itemsDeleted`.

### End-to-End Testing
`e2e/helpers/worktree-harness.ts` is **rewritten, not extended** (R12 AC 2). The current harness spawns the server with `cwd` set to this repository (`:200`), under which inference can never fire, and seeds `.spec-workflow` inside each worktree because it runs only in `--no-shared-worktree-specs` mode. The replacement sets the child `cwd` to the worktree, seeds `.spec-workflow` in the main repo for shared mode, and adds nested layouts alongside sibling ones.

Scenarios: two worktrees register as distinct projects sharing one spec list; a review triggered from worktree A diffs A's files and not B's; work logged in A but reviewed from B reports a mismatch and still produces a verdict; a worktree with unresolvable dependencies reports `workspace-deps-unresolvable` rather than a wall of fabricated errors; a documentation task whose logged paths live under `.spec-workflow` still produces a non-empty diff.
